  "enhanced_information": "Chlamydia trachomatis is an obligate intracellular pathogen, meaning it requires host cells for replication, which aligns with the consensus that it cannot survive outside of a host. In contrast, Neisseria gonorrhoeae and Streptococcus pneumoniae are classified as extracellular pathogens, as they do not require host cells for replication. Treponema pallidum is also not obligate intracellular, as it can survive outside a host. Mycobacterium tuberculosis is a facultative intracellular pathogen, capable of surviving within host cells but not strictly dependent on them for replication. Therefore, the only correct option for an obligate intracellular pathogen is Chlamydia trachomatis."
